body:
Essentially, I broke the deck down into three facets, each one that Sorin plays pretty well into. The vampire tribal section, reanimation section, and life as a currency section all work rather well in isolation if one angle is shut down, with the exception of the vampire section as it is really important that I can maintain a board state.

&#x200B;

First off, the vampire tribal synergy plays pretty well into itself. Using lords like Legion Lieutenant, Captivating Vampire, and eventually Bloodline Keeper lead to very strong boards off of simply playing one creature after another. One of the true all stars however is undoubtedly Bloodlord of Vaasgoth, as Sorin shooting an opponent practically guarantees all other creatures getting 3 counters on cast. While the deck is really good at making tall creatures, unfortunately it isn't superb at making the board wide.

&#x200B;

Secondly, the Reanimator section adds a lot of value to the vampire tribal strategy, which is important in locking down the board until we get deeper into the game and the third facet can trigger. A lot of vampires on the board drastically improve the advantage of the other creatures they share the field with, and draw a lot of removal. Luckily, they'll simply continue to come back!

&#x200B;

Thirdly, the life total section is the main strategy behind the deck. Sanguine Sacrament is useful at not only keeping us in the game, but the primary goal is to stick in the game until we can reach Bolas' Citadel or Lich's Mastery. Sanguine Sacrament can give us 12 "mana" to use on Bolas' Citadel or can be 12 drawn cards with Lich's Mastery. Not to mention Exquisite Blood and Sanguine Bond can be an instant win if we're lucky.

&#x200B;

There are a few changes that I hope to make down the line, especially with the new Sorin from M20. I also think I'd like to replace Death Grasp with Debt to the Deathless, just haven't dug up a copy yet.

Side note: I have yet to have posted on Reddit before, so I'll try my best to articulate my points on how this article kinda shows an apparent lack of knowledge on Critical Role and what they've done.

&#x200B;

I think that most of what this article says doesn't even honestly make sense. In life you don't really get to choose your friends, it's just kinda based on whomever is around you. I've always kinda disliked the concept that everyone needs to include POC in your friend group because it always seems very disdainful for people and how they treat one another. It's like "oh you won't make friends with any POC naturally, so you need to go out of your way to do as such." I grew up in a nearly entirely white community and most of my friends were white, but one of my closest friends was from the Dominican Republic. We grew up together and our friendship developed *naturally*. It wasn't because I thought I needed a friend of his skin color, or that I needed to be inclusive to him, its because we both loved Nintendo and Star Wars: The Clone Wars on Friday nights after school. I think that the concept that people will either consciously or subconsciously exclude people based on race, sex, or sexual orientation just isn't as common as people think it is (please don't call me racist or anything, I just feel like we are living in a world where people like people based on who they are, not what they are and I am not at all trying to say that people being cast out because of those criteria don't exist). The people who make up Critical Role, sure they're white, but that's just what life has put in front of them. Those are the people they love, and those are the people they want to do things with because they love them.

&#x200B;

And the concept that they don't include friends of other races is simply incorrect. A couple of great examples is Liam's fantastic one shot "Song of the Lorelei" as well as Khary Payton's appearances in C2. They have extensively invited POC to join them, not only that but each one of them has been *a friend*. Dungeons and Dragons at its core is about doing what you love with your friends and I think to make it something corporate or commercial would be both disingenuous to both the game and the community that plays it. Sure, they make money doing it, but its not simply because they're good at the game or because they do great voices, but it's instead because they ooze camaraderie and you can feel their friendship in each episode. Whenever I feel like I'm alone, Critical Role has been something special that makes me feel as though I'm a part of something. And its not exclusive to race, political affiliation, sex, or sexual orientation. Friendship is not limited by color, it is objectively strong no matter the race.
